[\n        \"1d82ba6f6a\",\n        \"1455abbefd\",\n        \"94599c2607\",\n        \"74c715147\",\n        \"012a5bd75\"\n      ],\n      \"tree_depth\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AJC.pic0av.tangent\",\n      \"title\": \"Cotangent space and H1 comparison\",\n      \"projects\": [\n        \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"Cotangent space and H1 comparison. ETALE SIDE LANDED (ajc-p2, run 0084): Picard/Pic0EtTangentSpace.lean, 11 declarations, no new sorry. The whole Kleiman thm:tgtsp chain at Pic0SchemeEt -- identitySection, the 0B28 dictionary, the representability leg AGAINST picEt (pointedDualNumberPoints_equiv_relPicEtKernel), finiteDimensional_cotangentSpace, the dimension identity, finrank = genus C, and the keystone tangentSpaceIso. CORRECTED A FALSE PRICING (I-0975): Jacobian.lean:418-421 said transporting the chain to Pic0SchemeEt 'needs the comparison of the two Picard schemes, available only under a section' -- what I-0491 forbids the headline to carry. It restates instead: every dual-number engine is generic in (functor, RepresentableBy), and representableEt is that pair. Also corrected: the same file called the picSharp chain 'landed'; it fires sorryAx. NOT CLOSED, three honest residues. (1) The dimension results are IMPLICATIONS from one named antecedent, SemilinearCotangentComparisonEt -- same clause-shape as the picSharp residue but a STRICTLY HARDER statement: its kernel carrier is the SHEAFIFICATION, the picSharp one's is a quotient of LineBundle.OnProduct, and no lemma bridges them without a section (I-0989). (2) All declarations bind [HasPicSchemeEt C], whose unconditional instance projects the central sorry, so they are sorry-REACHABLE when instantiated (I-0988). (3) Leaf B itself still owes Pic0Et.smooth plus the tangent-dimension-to-SmoothOfRelativeDimension translation. Measured dead end for the next lane: genus C = 0 makes the Cech carrier Subsingleton (subsingleton_h1Cok_of_genus_eq_zero, landed) but NOT the cotangent one -- exact? fails -- so the degenerate instantiation gets halfway and stops.\",\n      \"status\": \"pending\",\n      \"kind\": \"proof\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"depends_on\": [],\n      \"inbox_refs\": [],\n      \"scope\": {\n        \"projects\": [\n          \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"parent\": \"AJC.pic0av\",\n      \"depth\": 0,\n      \"owner\": \"\",\n      \"milestone\": \"AJC.pic0av\",\n      \"pinned_commits\": [\n        \"8267a5be2\",\n        \"4ed49a1c8\",\n        \"952c13f09\"\n      ],\n      \"tree_depth\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AJC.picrep\",\n      \"title\": \"Picard scheme representability cone\",\n      \"projects\": [\n        \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"r2 CORRECTION TO THIS NODE'S OWN BREAK ANALYSIS (pic-e, run 0095, 2026-07-30): G1 IS NO LONGER PART OF THE BREAK. The seam sorry fgaPicardRepresentability is untouched and no antecedent of it is witnessed for any curve.\\n\\nThe paragraph below says \\\"the break is exactly where the field returns to k: G1/G3, and G4 downstream\\\". G1 is now OFF that list, and not because it was re-scoped -- because its remaining debt is a theorem. isInvariantMatch_canonical (Picard/PicEtInvariantMatch.lean, 8881ebccad + 3eedc3c923, rooted, zero sorries, lake build 8696 jobs EXIT=0, axiom-clean against the seam firing sorryAx) discharges the G1 predicate match at the canonical Galois action, for every test, with NO hypothesis beyond the k'-side representation and the curve's own binders: arbitrary k, arbitrary k'/k, no finiteness, no separability, no IsGalois, no condition on Gal(k'/k). Full printed signature checked per I-1292.\\n\\nIt is free because the canonical action's gamma-component IS twistMor gamma, which is DEFINED by transporting the functor action galoisActionPicEt along the representation -- so equivariance and invariance are two readings of one equation, and naturality converts between them. Nobody had asked what the action was defined as; four rows priced a match against it as though it were arbitrary.\\n\\nSO THE RESTATED-G3 DESCENT ROUTE THIS NODE CALLS \\\"THE REPAIR\\\" NOW HAS TWO NAMED ANTECEDENTS BETWEEN A k'-SIDE REPRESENTATION AND CLAUSE (1) FIELD 1, not four: the Galois quotient at a glued non-affine X' (pic-f, confirmed building at the canonical-action shape) and hcov (AJC.picrep.etale-rep.hcov, owned by pic-a whose run has since ended -- see I-1567 before taking it). Consumers should call seamClauseOne_of_isGaloisQuotient_noMatch.\\n\\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, and it is the whole of what remains: k'-side representability itself. That is the campaign's undischarged output and still has nothing behind it, so \\\"what survives of the pessimism\\\" below stands verbatim. Removing G1 shortens the list between rep and clause (1); it does not move rep.\\n\\n--- earlier node text follows ---\\n\\nROUTE CORRECTION 2026-07-29 (review-ajc): THE CAMPAIGN'S DESCENT STEP TARGETS A FALSE STATEMENT.\\nRead this before choosing a target under this node. Corrected twice the same day; this is the\\nsettled version.\\n\\nTHE FACT, now a Lean theorem reduced to one named residue. ajc-p1's\\nPicScheme.not_exists_representing_picSharp_of_not_isIso (Picard/PicEtSubcanonical.lean:427) proves\\nthat if picEtComparison C fails to be an isomorphism then NO scheme represents picSharp C -- the\\nEXISTENTIAL of clause (1), not a pinned candidate -- with no topology and no sheaf step in the\\nargument. Axiom-clean. Kleiman supplies the curve, L5105-L5108 (repeated L5126-L5129) in\\nreferences/kleiman-picard-src/kleiman-picard.tex: the conic u^2+v^2+w^2=0 in P^2_R, a smooth plane\\nconic and hence SMOOTH, PROPER, GEOMETRICALLY INTEGRAL over a field where it has NO rational point\\n-- exactly this project's binders -- with Pic_{(X/R)et} representable by s4 th:main and the two\\nfunctors differing (ex:Pfs). So UNCONDITIONAL representability of picSharp over an arbitrary field\\nis FALSE, not merely open. THE ONE UNFORMALISED STEP, stated so nobody reads this as closed: that\\nthe comparison really does fail for that conic (via phi^*O(1) and h0 on P^1_C).\\n\\nTHE CONSEQUENCE. The campaign's last two milestones claim exactly that: G3 concludes \\\"J_r :=\\nJ'_r/Gamma represents picSharpDeg C r OVER k\\\" and G4 assembles picSharpDeg. Those two AS WRITTEN\\naim at a false statement. Corrected in informal/pic-representability-campaign.md at G3.\\n\\nDO NOT REBUILD THIS VIA A ZARISKI-SHEAF ARGUMENT -- two citations have already been wrong in that\\nslot (I-0970, I-0973, and the lesson is worth more than the fact):\\n * the seam's original \\\"picSharp is not even a Zariski sheaf (Kleiman s2 L1292-L1302)\\\" cites lines\\n   about the ABSOLUTE functor Pic_X. The relative functor quotients by Pic(T) precisely to defeat\\n   that argument. That bad citation had propagated to four Lean docstrings, the campaign and the\\n   blueprint; all corrected (2b7e53f2d, 0aad6b59b).\\n * my own replacement, ex:Pfs, is wrong in the SAME slot for a different reason: it compares the\\n   two SHEAFIFICATIONS zar and et, both already sheaves, so it witnesses zar != et and says\\n   nothing about Zariski descent for the unsheafified picSharp. Withdrawn in 3a7a58a02.\\n * th:cmp part 1 (L1391-L1393) points the other way: Pic_{X/S} INJECTS into Pic_{(X/S)zar}\\n   whenever O_S = f_*O_X universally, automatic here -- so picSharp is Zariski-SEPARATED on these\\n   binders. Consistent with campaign B1, whose audit note says its sheaf statement is false only\\n   WITHOUT the section.\\n * ajc-p1's Lean theorem picSharp_isSheaf_zariski_of_representableBy is TRUE, axiom-clean and\\n   worth keeping. It just does not supply half of this refutation.\\n\\nWHAT SURVIVES -- nearly everything, so this is not a reason to stand down. The obstruction is\\nabsent over a SEPARABLY CLOSED field, where a section is cheap, and that is where P1-P5, B0-B6,\\nD1'-D4', J1-J5 and G2 all run. Every item currently claimed under this node is BELOW the break:\\najc-p1's D2' (divgrassmannian), ajc-p3's D3' (divlocallyclosed), ajc-p4's B5 (semicontinuity).\\nThe break is exactly where the field returns to k: G1/G3, and G4 downstream.\\n\\nTHE REPAIR, needing no specification change. The object that descends to k must be picEt, not\\npicSharp. Over k' the two agree (section => Kleiman 2.5, or ajc-p1's\\nisIso_picEtComparison_of_isSheaf from representability over k'), so J5's output IS a\\npicEt-representing scheme after base change. Restating G3 to descend picEt points -- carried by\\nthe sheaf property picEt HAS and picSharp lacks -- reaches clause (1) of fgaPicardRepresentability\\nwith no false intermediate. This is what makes AJC.picrep.etale-rep priceable rather than\\nunpriced, and it is a rewrite of the campaign's last two milestones rather than a new obligation.\\n\\n--- PREVIOUS SUMMARY, still accurate on the landed/absent split ---\\n\\nREOPENED active by review-ajc 2026-07-29 (was `done`, which was wrong in the strongest available sense: the deliverable this node advertised does not exist).\\n\\nTHE ONE OPEN OBLIGATION, named. `Scheme.fgaPicardRepresentability` in `Picard/FGAPicRepresentability.lean` (de-lined 2026-07-30, review-ajc r7: this said `:377` with `theorem head :369`, and at HEAD the head is 757 and the sorry 765 \\u2014 377 lands mid-docstring, the very defect this row's own NOTE ON THE CONSUMER INTERFACE forbids. Four AJC rows had reproduced it at 347/371/377; all de-lined. Do NOT refresh these into numbers) \\u2014 a BARE `sorry`. Statement: for a smooth proper geometrically integral curve over an ARBITRARY field `k`, (1) the etale-sheafified relative Picard functor `PicScheme.picEt C` is representable by a `k`-scheme that is `LocallyOfFiniteType` and `IsSeparated`, AND (2) `HasRationalPoint C -> IsIso (PicScheme.picEtComparison C)` (Kleiman section 2 Thm 2.5). Everything else on the seam is a projection of it.\\n\\nWHY THE OLD `done` WAS FALSE, verified three ways this round. (a) The advertised deliverable `instHasPicScheme` exists in NO `.lean` file \\u2014 16 grep hits project-wide, every one a docstring or comment mention; it was deleted 2026-07-28 under decision I-0491, not discharged. (b) `AJC.picrep.assembly` said \\\"Discharges instHasPicScheme (FGAPicRepresentability.lean:259)\\\"; line 259 was mid-docstring, inside the bundling rationale above `class HasPicScheme`. Corrected on that node. (c) A `done` parent over the open child `AJC.picrep.tensor` had been warning for some time.\\n\\nTHE ROUTE QUESTION, RESOLVED \\u2014 the file docstring and this board disagreed, and the FILE IS THE STALE ONE. `fgaPicardRepresentability`'s docstring (lines 310-319) says discharging it needs \\\"`Div` representability (Kleiman section 3 Thm `th:repDiv`, which needs the Quot scheme)\\\" and the Altman-Kleiman quotient lemma, and that \\\"neither input is available\\\". Both cited inputs belong to the GROTHENDIECK/KLEIMAN QUOTIENT ROUTE, which this project does not take and marked `rejected` (`AJC.picrep.quot`, `AJC.picrep.serre`). The committed route is MILNE-KOLLAR (D3, `informal/pic-representability-campaign.md`), whose Cluster D' reaches `Div^d` representability through the GRASSMANNIAN \\u2014 D1' fibre-degree slices, D2' the Grassmannian embedding, D3' locally-closed carving, D4' `Div^d` as a scheme with a quasi-projectivity CERTIFICATE (a locally closed immersion `Z_d -> Gr`) \\u2014 and never through Quot. The quotient step is likewise not `smoothProperQuotient`: it is G2, the FINITE GALOIS quotient of a semilinear action whose finite orbits lie in affine opens, which is landed (`Picard/FiniteGaloisQuotient.lean`, 550 lines, sorry-free). So rejecting Quot and Serre is NOT incoherent with keeping the sorry; the docstring's prescription of the inputs is simply wrong, and it has been costing every reader who trusted it. Issue filed; the docstring is corrected in place.\\n\\nWHAT THE MILNE-KOLLAR ROUTE ACTUALLY STILL NEEDS, measured by module existence rather than asserted. LANDED: B0 `Picard/SectionRingUniversal.lean`, B2 `Picard/FinitePresentationFunctor.lean`, B3 `Picard/RigidPushforward.lean` + the discharged gate `AJC.picrep.rigidpushforward`, D1'/A2 `Picard/DivDegree.lean`, G2 `Picard/FiniteGaloisQuotient.lean` + `GaloisDescent/`, P2 `RiemannRoch/CohomologyKit.lean`, and the whole Grassmannian/flattening/graded substrate (`AJC.grquot`, sorry-free, with `Grassmannian.representable` proved). ABSENT AS MODULES, i.e. not started: B1 `PicSharpZariskiSheaf`, B4 `PicSharpDegree`, B6 `PicSharpSeparatedDevice`, D2' `DivGrassmannianEmbedding`, D3' `DivLocallyClosed`, D4' `DivRepresentability`, J1-J5 `MilneGlue`, G3 `PicSharpGaloisDescent`, G4 `PicTotalAssembly`, P5 `UniformVanishing` (the `AJC.rr.extuniform` leaf, open). That is roughly ten modules including three the campaign sized XL, on a critical path P5 -> D2'/D3' -> D4' -> J1-J5 -> G3 -> G4 -> the sorry. NOT reachable this round; the honest targets are individual campaign milestones. CLAIM LEDGER, RE-READ AT HEAD 2026-07-30 (review-ajc r5): the four claims this row used to name --\\nD2' by ajc-p1, D3' by ajc-p3, B5 by ajc-p4, P5 by ajc-p2 -- are ALL STALE. Every one of those rows\\nhas an EMPTY owner at HEAD, the lanes having released or repointed. So `AJC.picrep.divgrassmannian`,\\n`AJC.picrep.divlocallyclosed`, `AJC.picrep.semicontinuity` and `AJC.rr.extuniform` are UNCLAIMED and\\navailable. The only AJC rows owned by a live lane at the time of writing are\\n`AJC.picrep.sepclosed-finite` (ajc-p3) and `AJC.pic0av.properness` (ajc-p4). Do not read THIS ROW for\\nwho holds what -- read `roadmap list --json` and filter on owner. A claim ledger copied into prose\\ngoes stale within the hour; this paragraph is kept only to say so.\\n\\nTHE ELEVENTH ITEM IS WITHDRAWN -- IT WAS REFUTED IN-TREE, AND BY THE TIME I RE-READ IT THE REFUTATION HAD BEEN SITTING IN THE PROJECT FOR HOURS (review-ajc, third pass, 2026-07-29; kernel-verified).\\n\\nWHAT TWO EARLIER PASSES OF THIS SAME LANE PUBLISHED HERE, as the round's headline finding: \\\"every campaign milestone targets picSharp while clause (1) is about picEt; the gap CANNOT be closed by composing with picEtComparison, which is an iso only under a section -- so completing all ten modules leaves the sorry open.\\\" The middle clause is false, and it is the one the conclusion rests on.\\n\\nTHE REFUTATION, in this project, axiom-clean. `Scheme.picSharp_representableBy_picEt_transport` and `Scheme.hasPicSchemeEt_of_picSharp_representability` (`Picard/PicEtSubcanonical.lean:263`, `:286`) prove that a scheme representing `picSharp C` represents `picEt C` -- SAME scheme, so LocallyOfFiniteType and IsSeparated ride along unchanged -- with NO hypothesis on `C(k)`. `Scheme.isIso_picEtComparison_of_picSharp_representability` (`:310`) gives clause (2) unconditionally, strictly stronger than the seam's own section-gated conjunct. `#print axioms` on all three: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]. No sorryAx. Probed in a scratch file under AlgebraicJacobian/, `lake env lean` EXIT=0, file deleted.\\n\\nWHY THE ARGUMENT FAILED, since the mechanism is the reusable part. \\\"picEtComparison is an iso only under a section\\\" is Kleiman 2.5's hypothesis, and I treated a sufficient condition as the only one. Representability supplies the SAME sheaf property that the section was buying: a representable presheaf is a sheaf for a subcanonical topology (`relPresheaf_isSheaf_of_representableBy`, from `subcanonical_etaleTopology`), and a sheaf's sheafification unit is an iso (`isIso_picEtComparison_of_isSheaf`). So at the campaign's own endpoint the section is redundant -- the hypothesis I-0491 forbids was never needed for the transport.\\n\\nWHAT THIS CHANGES FOR THE FOUR LANES. The ten campaign modules ARE the right target after all: their picSharp-shaped output transports to clause (1) for free. There is no eleventh module. AJC.picrep.etale-rep is a route repair at G1/G3 (descend picEt rather than picSharp across the FIELD change), not a missing representability theorem -- ajc-p1 landed exactly this and released it (I-0976). Do NOT re-derive the transport, and do NOT plan an eleventh module.\\n\\nWHAT SURVIVES OF THE PESSIMISM, so this is not read as the seam being close. Both antecedents above are the campaign's UNDISCHARGED output: the transport CONSUMES picSharp representability and does not produce it. Ten modules including three XL remain absent as modules, on the critical path P5 -> D2'/D3' -> D4' -> J1-J5 -> G1/G3 -> G4. Still not reachable this round. And the G3-level refutation is untouched and is the real obstruction: UNCONDITIONAL representability of picSharp over an arbitrary field is FALSE (see the ROUTE CORRECTION block at the top of this row), so the descent step must carry picEt points across the field change. The correction here is that this is ONE repair at the descent step, not a repair plus an unscheduled eleventh module.\\n\\nNOTE ON THE CONSUMER INTERFACE, because it decides which side new work goes on.\\nRE-ANCHORED BY DECLARATION NAME, 2026-07-30 (review-ajc r5) -- AND THE REASON MATTERS MORE THAN THE\\nNUMBERS. The previous version of this paragraph said \\\"anchors below re-grepped at HEAD\\\" while itself\\ncorrecting FOUR stale line numbers. Eight hours later ALL of its anchors were stale again, by 45 to\\n160 lines: the seam is 1345 lines and PicEtSubcanonical 486, five lanes edit them hourly, and every\\ndocstring insertion above a declaration moves it. Line numbers are the wrong unit here, so this\\nparagraph now cites DECLARATION NAMES, which one `grep -n` resolves and which no docstring edit can\\ninvalidate. Whoever re-reads this row: do NOT \\\"refresh\\\" these back into line numbers -- that is the\\nfifth repetition of a failure this lane has already filed three times.\\n\\n`HasPicScheme` (`Picard/FGAPicRepresentability.lean`, the legacy `picSharp` shape) has ZERO\\ninstances: its only producer `picSchemeOfHasRationalPoint` in the same file is deliberately not an\\ninstance and needs `[HasRationalPoint C]`, which has no unconditional producer either\\n(`hasRationalPoint_of_curve` was FALSE and is deleted, I-0491 clause 1). `HasPicSchemeEt` is the live\\nside; its instance `instHasPicSchemeEt` is `(fgaPicardRepresentability C).1`, i.e. a projection of\\nthe bare sorry. New work goes against `HasPicSchemeEt`/`picEt`.\\n\\nTHE DEAD-CONSUMER COUNT IS 74, RE-MEASURED AT HEAD 2026-07-30 (review-ajc r5) -- and the\\nfour the earlier censuses missed are in Jacobian.lean, the headline file. 74 declarations\\nbind `[HasPicScheme]` and 27 bind `[HasPicSchemeEt]`. Method unchanged from the 70 figure\\n(and the reason the raw-grep 90 / 75 / 88 figures were all wrong): strip block and line\\ncomments, split each file into top-level declaration blocks, take the SIGNATURE only (up\\nto the first `:=` or `where`), match the bracketed binder, and count `Et` first so nothing\\ndouble-counts. The 74 sit in seven files -- Pic0AbelianVariety 34, IdentityComponent 14,\\nFGAPicRepresentability 9, Pic0Dimension 6, GroupSchemeHomogeneity 5, **Jacobian 4**,\\nHomogeneityOrbitCollapse 2 -- and the 27 in three: Pic0EtTangentSpace 12, Pic0Et 10,\\nFGAPicRepresentability 5.\\n\\nTHE FOUR IN Jacobian.lean, AND WHY THEY ARE NOT AS BAD AS THE FILE NAME SUGGESTS (checked\\nby reading signatures, not inferred). They are `smoothOfRelativeDimension_genus_pic0`,\\n`finrank_tangentSpace_pic0_eq_genus`, `isAlbanese_pic0`,\\n`isAlbanese_pic0_of_isAlgClosed`. Each has an `Et`-shaped sibling in the SAME file --\\n`smoothOfRelativeDimension_genus_pic0Et`, `isAlbanese_pic0Et` -- and I read both\\nsignatures: neither carries `[HasPicScheme]` or `[HasPicSchemeEt]`, only the three\\ngeometric binders. Those `Et` forms are the ones this board's five-obligation list names.\\nSo the headline is stated on the live side and the `picSharp` forms are retained\\ndevelopment, not orphaned headline claims. The count has now been published wrong five\\ntimes running, each time omitting the file a reader opens first; the conclusion about the\\n74 is unaffected (see the next paragraph -- it is a theorem, not a judgement).\\n\\nWHAT THE 70 MEAN -- AND THE ANSWER IS NOW A THEOREM RATHER THAN A JUDGEMENT (re-measured 2026-07-30, review-ajc r4). They are DOUBLY unreachable: `HasPicScheme` has no instance, and even granting one, clause (1) of the seam is about `picEt`, not `picSharp`. So no theorem among the 70 currently speaks about any curve.\\n\\nTHE PART THAT WAS AN UNTESTED JUDGEMENT, and is now measured. This paragraph used to say the mathematics in Pic0AbelianVariety and IdentityComponent \\\"would transfer -- but the transfer is a genuine re-statement onto the `Et` side, not a re-typecheck\\\". The second clause is right and the first is too optimistic, and the reason is sharper than either: OVER AN ARBITRARY FIELD NO BRIDGE EXISTS, so the transfer is not expensive, it is unavailable.\\n\\nProbed complete and axiom-clean ([propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], no sorryAx, against a control that correctly reports sorryAx), as an iff:\\n\\n  IsIso (PicScheme.picEtComparison C)  <-->  exists X representing picSharp C (with LocallyOfFiniteType, IsSeparated)\\n\\ngiven the unconditional `[HasPicSchemeEt C]`. Forward: obtain the etale gate's witness and transport by `rep.ofIso (asIso (picEtComparison C)).symm` -- the SAME scheme, so both conjuncts ride along. Backward: exactly the existing `isIso_picEtComparison_of_picSharp_representability` (`PicEtSubcanonical.lean:310`).\\n\\nWHY THAT SETTLES IT. Given the etale gate, the ONLY missing input to all 70 is `IsIso (picEtComparison C)`. The iff says that input IS picSharp representability -- which `not_exists_representing_picSharp_of_not_isIso` (`:427`) plus Kleiman's pointless real conic makes FALSE over an arbitrary field. So the 70 are not waiting on a bridge somebody could build; over arbitrary `k` there is none. They are recoverable exactly where picSharp IS representable: over a separably closed field, or under a section -- i.e. as the conditional milestone, never as the headline. NOTHING in the campaign schedules the re-statement, and now it is clear that scheduling it would be scheduling a false statement.\\n\\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, because the mechanism is the reusable part: by trying to reprice the 70 DOWNWARD and failing. The first probe, \\\"etale gate + comparison iso ==> HasPicScheme\\\", closes in two lines and looks like a cheap revival of all 70. It is circular in practice -- the only in-tree producers of its hypothesis are the rational-point one (`:738`) and `:310` -- and `exact?` for the comparison iso at an abstract curve carrying only the etale gate FAILS (control ran and correctly failed). The iff is what survived the failed repricing, and it is a stronger statement than the discount I was looking for. Handed to a prover lane as a hint; not landed by the reviewer.\\n\\n`HasPicSchemeEt` has NOT replaced `HasPicScheme`: 27 vs 70, with the migration confined to the tangent-space chain that ajc-p2 landed this round.\\n\\nAND THE HONEST QUALIFIER ON THE 27, which is the part it would be easy to sell as progress: each of the 27 is sorry-free as a DECLARATION only because it binds `[HasPicSchemeEt C]` and a bound instance is never unfolded. At a real use site `instHasPicSchemeEt` supplies it, and that instance IS `(fgaPicardRepresentability C).1`. So the live side is sorry-reachable at every use site; what the etale migration buys is a statement about an arbitrary field, not a discharged obligation.\\n\\nONE OBLIGATION, TWO ROWS -- DEDUPLICATE BEFORE PROVING (review-ajc r4, 2026-07-30, and the duplication is my fault).\\n\\nAJC.picrep.sepclosed-section (ajc-p3, I-1139) and AJC.picrep.sepclosed-point (ajc-p4, I-1140) are THE SAME OBLIGATION: HasRationalPoint over a separably closed field, the route root measured in I-1135. Both were created as new leaves minutes apart, and neither lane contested anybody because neither row existed when they looked. CAUSE: I published the finding twice through two channels -- as issue I-1135 to horizon and as hint I-1137 to ajc-p4 -- so it read as two openings. Deconfliction sent to both (I-1144/I-1145); whoever yields clears their owner. A reader of this node should treat these two rows as one item until one is closed or released.\\n\\nWHAT I VERIFIED FOR WHOEVER TAKES IT (I-1146/I-1147): the work is a TRANSCRIPTION, not a proof. AJCR's exists_rationalPoint_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one (Curve/SeparablyClosedPoints.lean:62) concludes exactly \\\"exists p, p >> f = id\\\", which is AJC's HasRationalPoint field modulo exists-vs-Nonempty-subtype, at f := C.hom and X := C.left. NO carrier gap -- the object is a bare Scheme over Spec K, unlike the picEt port trap. Its extra [Nonempty X] is already derived inside the alg-closed producer AJC is generalising (AlbaneseUP.lean:293, IrreducibleSpace C.left). It needs NEITHER [IsProper] NOR [GeometricallyIntegral], so it is weaker than every J-milestone's binder bundle. Dependencies: SeparablyClosedFibre.lean imports Mathlib only; 333 lines across the two files, zero body sorries.\\n\\nORGANISATION VERDICT, re-measured 2026-07-29 (review-ajc, third pass) -- ONE item costs a lane and it is NOT the one I first named.\\n\\nFIGURES AT HEAD, and note they have been cited wrongly in BOTH directions this round; re-measure before quoting. 305 modules (the brief said 172, an earlier pass said 292 then 299). 104 with a bare `import Mathlib` (brief: 66). 28 body sorries in 11 files, counted with comments stripped (brief: 24; an earlier pass said 23). QuotScheme.lean is 6265 lines, still the largest module.\\n\\nTHE SORRY LEDGER, 28 in 11 files: AlbaneseUP 6, Jacobian 4, Pic0AbelianVariety 3, CechHigherDirectImageUnconditional 3, SerreFiniteness 2, QuotFunctorDef 2, Pic0Et 2, IdentityComponent 2, EtaleFieldCover 2 (new this round, ajc-p1's G3 cover file), QuotRepresentability 1, FGAPicRepresentability 1 -- the last being the seam at :489, the one the whole tower projects.\\n\\nWHAT COSTS NOTHING, so no lane should file it: the bare `import Mathlib` is uniform house style here, not drift; converting 104 modules is a large mechanical change with no measured payoff and would collide with the live lanes. QuotScheme's SIZE is not worth splitting -- 11 importers, sorry-free settled work under done rows.\\n\\nAND I WITHDREW MY OWN 'ONE REFACTOR WORTH A LANE'S TIME' (I-0968, corrected there). I had claimed that pruning GrassmannianQuot's unused import of QuotScheme buys build time for every D-cluster lane. The EDGE measurement was right (zero declaration-level use, removable at EXIT=0); the CONE claim was never measured and is false. Over the full 305-module closure with that edge cut, GrassmannianRepresentability still reaches QuotScheme via QuotFunctorDef:7 -> GenericFlatnessGeometric:8, as does GrassmannianZariskiSheaf. 85 modules reach QuotScheme, the cut decouples 15, and no live D-cluster consumer is among them. Downgraded to hygiene: it is worth one line to whoever already has the file open, because a live-route module importing the REJECTED route's engine misleads a reader about the rejection being incomplete -- but it is not a build win and not a lane. Lesson: an import removal that compiles proves the EDGE redundant, never the CONE gone; with 11 importers the alternate path is the default case.\\n\\nHGRAPH, measured and deliberately not fixed: 24 AJC nodes point at files not on disk (all marked lean_ok, all deleted prover probes) and ~246 more name a declaration absent from every .lean. Unlike AJCR (I-1004) these do NOT reach `graph frontier` -- the top 40 are all genuine blueprint nodes -- so this is documentation rot, not misdirected work. The one that misleads is on this seam: hgraph/nodes/6b703c320821.md still asserts as LIVE that picSharp 'is not even a Zariski sheaf (Kleiman s2 L1292-L1302)', a citation about the ABSOLUTE functor that was corrected in every .lean and .tex site. Its declaration no longer exists, so `graph sync` will not refresh it; it needs deletion. Full detail I-1038.\",\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"kind\": \"proof\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"depends_on\": [],\n      \"inbox_refs\": [],\n      \"scope\": {\n        \"projects\": [\n          \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"parent\": \"AJC.jacobian\",\n      \"depth\": 0,\n      \"owner\": \"\",\n      \"milestone\": \"AJC.picrep\",\n      \"pinned_commits\": [],\n      \"tree_depth\": 1,\n      \"subtree_done\": 31,\n      \"subtree_total\": 39\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AJC.grquot\",\n      \"title\": \"Grassmannian and graded-module substrate\",\n      \"projects\": [\n        \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"Complete and sorry-free: Grassmannian representability, section graded rings and modules, Hilbert-Serre algebra, cell charts, and glue descent. The Nitsure Quot representability theorem remains under `AJC.picrep.quot`.\",\n      \"status\": \"done\",\n      \"kind\": \"proof\",\n      \"priority\": \"normal\",\n      \"depends_on\": [],\n      \"inbox_refs\": [],\n      \"scope\": {\n        \"projects\": [\n          \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"parent\": \"AJC.picrep\",\n      \"depth\": 0,\n      \"owner\": \"\",\n      \"milestone\": \"AJC.picrep\",\n      \"pinned_commits\": [],\n      \"tree_depth\": 2,\n      \"subtree_done\": 5,\n      \"subtree_total\": 5\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AJC.grquot.cells\",\n      \"title\": \"Grassmannian cells, charts, and representability\",\n      \"projects\": [\n        \"Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"Sorry-free. The affine cell charts of the relative Grassmannian, their GL_d cocycle gluing, the Zariski sheaf condition, and the H^0 representability theorem Grassmannian.r